  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1235 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\nLow pressure tracks east from Manitoba Canada Friday night into\nnorthern Ontario / Hudson Bay later this weekend. A cold front \nand potent 500mb trough extending well south of this low will \ntrack east across southern WI late Friday night into Saturday \nmorning, with some ~10% chances for light precip in the forecast\n(barely a hundredth of an inch QPF on most model guidance). Rain\nis the favored precip type if applicable (given some slight warm-\nnoses evident on forecast soundings), though a few snowflakes \ncan't be ruled out. The associated cloud cover is expected to\nhinder the diurnal cooling cycle, with lows of only ~30 degrees \nin the forecast, trending warmer with each forecast issuance. Daytime\nhighs rise into the low 50s for Saturday, marking the warmest day\nin the forecast period. \n\nA separate 500mb trough and surface low pressure system will track\nfrom the southern Great Plains Saturday past the southern tip of \nIllinois midday Sunday. 20-50% rain chances are forecast with \nthis system across southern WI from Saturday night through \nSunday, with the highest chances towards the SE corner of the \nstate. A few wet snowflakes could mix in at times, though 2m air\ntemperatures and surface / ground temperatures are looking too \nwarm for any accumulation. Rain remains the favored precip type \ngiven the antecedent warmth. Dry weather is then expected \nthrough the early half of the work week as high pressure settles\ninto the region. \n\nSheppard",
